{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1500108583,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDExNjUyMzM0MTU="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1500108591,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MTE2NTIzMzQzMg=="},"title":"Why do we need to instruct GitPython to clean up?","was":"Why do we need to instruct GitPython to clean up?"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1500123438,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTUzMjMzMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315532331"},"message":"Hm... Iirc it is a line of code in wrapt where it segfaults at least in some cases. We don't have other extensions which relate and python code mustn't segfault.\nAs for those batched processes, I remember them haunting me, but forgot what was the evil outcome of them sticking around","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1500136574,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTU0NTg2Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315545862"},"message":"re wrapt -- kinda of a proof: with my [PR disabling extension-based implementation](https://github.com/GrahamDumpleton/wrapt/pull/104) -- does not segfault\n```shell\n(venv-regular) root@ae4a1f1452ef:/tmp/datalad# `which python` -m nose -s -v  datalad.distribution.tests.test_publish\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_gh1426 ... .: target(+) [/tmp/datalad_temp_test_gh1426jqm9z2 (git)]\nok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_invalid_call ... ok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_publish_recursive ... Fatal Python error: GC object already tracked\nAborted (core dumped)\n\n\n(venv-regular) root@ae4a1f1452ef:/tmp/datalad# WRAPT_DISABLE_EXTENSIONS=1 `which python` -m nose -s -v  datalad.distribution.tests.test_publish\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_gh1426 ... .: target(+) [/tmp/datalad_temp_test_gh1426N_S1Pv (git)]\nok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_invalid_call ... ok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_publish_recursive ... ok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_publish_simple ... ok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_publish_with_data ... ok\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_publish_depends ... SKIP: Run this test by setting DATALAD_TESTS_SSH\ndatalad.distribution.tests.test_publish.test_smth_about_not_supported ... SKIP: Run this test by setting DATALAD_TESTS_SSH\nVersions: cmd:annex=6.20170525+gitge1cf095ae-1~ndall+1 cmd:git=2.11.0 cmd:system-git=2.11.0 cmd:system-ssh=7.4p1 git=2.1.3 tqdm=4.11.2\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 7 tests in 83.485s\n\nOK (SKIP=2)\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1500281907,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTcwMDU3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315700570"},"message":"To be honest I don't fully remember all the implications. But: We did get in trouble at some point, because of GitPython's git (-batch) processes still running, when we didn't have the original `*Repo` instance anymore.  I forgot the circumstances leading to the issue, but at some point we did get masses of such hanging processes connected to invalid repositories. The commit did solve the issue. \nThere's a comment by @yarikoptic in PR #1091 somewhat pointing that out, but it's not very telling. I'm sure there has been a dedicated discussion on that, where Yarik was complaining about all those processes, but I wasn't able to find it yet ... :-/\n\nThe call to `precommit` was to make sure we flush in memory changes to the index before deleting it. We didn't actually have those kind of changes, I think. But it's possibile to have them with GitPython, therefore better call it. Additionally `precommit` was overwritten by `AnnexRepo` to also close `git-annex'` batched commands, IIRC.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1500282136,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTcwMTMyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315701324"},"message":"Re wrapt:\n\nI hereby want to confess, that I still don't belive it's actually wrapt's fault, too. The segfault might happen in there and this might be a bug in wrapt itself, but I'm pretty sure that our use of it is somehow wrong or very unusual at some point - leading to the condition, wrapt isn't able to deal with.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1500290524,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTcyODkwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315728907"},"message":"I don't know when other proof you guys need - pure Python implementation doesn't segfault, wrapt is in the traceback... Are you guys sure you aren't a president?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1500301778,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTc3MjQ1NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315772454"},"message":"I don't dispute it's wrapt, that is segfaulting. And yes: Python mustn't segfault. But this still can mean, that wrapt should lead to an exception instead of a segfault. In that sense there's a bug in wrapt - okay. But then this still wouldn't be the actual issue we have to solve. I simply suspect there is such an issue with our code. Otherwise a lot of people would experience that segfault, too. So, what I mean is, that I think it's likely to be two bugs. One within wrapt and one within our code. And if this is the case, then fixing the wrapt bug wouldn't fix ours. But fixing ours would make the wrapt's one irrelevant to us. Do you understand, what I mean?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1500308718,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTgwNDk4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315804985"},"message":"\u003e I simply suspect there is such an issue with our code.\n\nI bet we do have some issues left, can't argue ;)  Either any is related to the segfault and need to be fixed -- not sure ;) segfault is really a sign of non-sanctioned behavior/faulty logic in C code.  Finding a bug in our code which might hide wrapt bug, and again assuming that \"oh -- wrapt bug is not that important since we do not see it any longer\" is just again to postpone/delay pain and dealing with it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1500308786,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMxNTgwNTI5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-315805296"},"message":"unrelated -- I am the master of segfaults!\n```shell\n$\u003e git merge origin/master\n[1]    4335 segmentation fault  git merge origin/master\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503993843,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNTU4NjU5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642#issuecomment-325586590"},"message":"Putting this on record.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503993843,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTIyNTExOTE3Nw=="},"status":2}]}